\subsection*{§2. Chain Conditions in Finite Module Domains}

The module theorem by which the chain conditions are transferred occurs, in the
application given here, only for modules of an extension ring. Since the proof is
the same in the case of a general module domain, such a domain will be taken as
the starting point.

A system $M$ of elements $\alpha,\beta,\ldots$ is called a module domain with
respect to a ring $\mR$ if two operations are given in $M$, each leading uniquely to
elements of $M$: an additive combination of the elements and a multiplication of
the elements by elements of $\mR$; if $M$ is an Abelian group with respect to addition,
while multiplication satisfies the associative law; and if the distributive law holds
in both forms.\footnote{Compare \emph{Ideal Theory}, §9.}

For a module domain, all module definitions given in §1, 1. that do not refer to
multiplication plainly remain valid. In particular, $M$ is called a finite module
domain if there are finitely many elements $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_k$ of $M$ such that
$M$ equals the module derived from $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_k$, hence equals the system
of all linear forms $r_1\xi_1+\cdots+r_k\xi_k$ when $\mR$ has an identity element;
otherwise additional terms $n_i\xi_i$ occur (compare note~8).

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Module theorem.} \emph{Let $M$ be a finite module domain with respect
to a commutative ring $\mR$ with identity element, and suppose that in $\mR$ the
divisor- respectively multiple-chain condition for ideals holds. Then in $M$ the
divisor- respectively multiple-chain condition for modules holds.}

The proof rests on assigning uniquely to every module in $M$ a system of finitely
many ideals from $\mR$ in such a way that to a proper divisor respectively proper
multiple of the module there corresponds each time at least one proper divisor
respectively multiple ideal.

An element of $M$ is said to have length $i$ if it can be written in at least one
way as a linear form in $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_i$, while it has no representation as a
linear form in $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_{i-1}$. To an arbitrary module $\mA$ in $M$ assign
now $k$ ideals $\mathfrak a_1,\ldots,\mathfrak a_k$ from $\mR$: let $\mA_i$ mean
the module of all elements of $\mA$ of length $\le i$, and let $\mathfrak a_i$ denote
the system of all coefficients of $\xi_i$ in $\mA_i$. First one obtains:

If $\mB$ is a proper divisor of $\mA$, and if $\mathfrak b_1,\ldots,\mathfrak b_k$ are
the ideals assigned to $\mB$, then among the $\mathfrak b_i$ there is at least one
proper divisor of the corresponding $\mathfrak a_i$. For from
$\mA\equiv0\pmod{\mB}$ it follows that $\mA_\lambda\equiv0\pmod{\mB_\lambda}$, and hence
$\mathfrak a_\lambda\equiv0\pmod{\mathfrak b_\lambda}$ for $\lambda=1,2,\ldots,k$. By hypothesis
there must be elements in $\mB$ not contained in $\mA$, and hence such elements of
smallest length $i$. Then $\mathfrak b_i$ is a proper divisor of
$\mathfrak a_i$; indeed
$\mathfrak b_i\equiv0\pmod{\mathfrak b_i}$ but
$\mathfrak b_i\not\equiv0\pmod{\mathfrak a_i}$ when
\[
        \beta=b_1\xi_1+\cdots+b_i\xi_i
\]
is such an element of smallest length in $\mB$. If
$\mathfrak b_i\equiv0\pmod{\mathfrak a_i}$, there exists an element
\[
        \alpha=a_1\xi_1+\cdots+b_i\xi_i\equiv0\pmod{\mA},
\]
and therefore an element $\beta-\alpha\equiv0\pmod{\mB}$,
$\beta-\alpha\not\equiv0\pmod{\mA}$, of smaller length than $\beta$.

Now let a divisor or multiple chain
\[
        \mA_1,\mA_2,\ldots,\mA_r,\ldots
\]
be given; suppose the corresponding chain condition holds in $\mR$. If
$\mathfrak a_{r1},\ldots,\mathfrak a_{rk}$ are the ideals assigned to
$\mA_r$, then the sequences
\[
        \mathfrak a_{1\lambda},\mathfrak a_{2\lambda},\ldots,
        \mathfrak a_{r\lambda},\ldots
\]
are divisor respectively multiple chains for $\lambda=1,\ldots,k$. Hence by hypothesis
there is an index $\mu$ such that the ideal $\mathfrak a_{\mu\lambda}$ is equal to
all subsequent ideals for every $\lambda$. But then, by what was just proved, the module
$\mA_\mu$ is equal to all subsequent modules; thus the chain conditions are
transferred.

This immediately gives the following:

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Consequence of the module theorem.} \emph{If in $\mR$ the multiple-chain
condition holds modulo every ideal different from the zero ideal, then in $M$ the
multiple-chain condition holds modulo every module $\mC$ whose assigned ideals
$\mathfrak c_1,\ldots,\mathfrak c_k$ are all different from the zero ideal.}
Under
these hypotheses the multiple chains
$\mathfrak a_{1\lambda},\ldots,\mathfrak a_{r\lambda},\ldots$ terminate after finitely many
steps.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Additional remark.} If $\mR$ is a ring without identity element, then
the divisor-chain condition and the multiple-chain condition modulo ideals different
from the zero ideal are still transferred. Namely, instead of $\mA$ one considers the
system $\overline{\mA}$ of all elements of the form
\[
        a_1\xi_1+\cdots+a_k\xi_k+n_1\xi_{k+1}+\cdots+n_k\xi_{2k},
\]
which again passes into $\mA$ when $\xi_{k+\lambda}$ is replaced by $\xi_\lambda$.
To this $\overline{\mA}$ one can assign, correspondingly as
above, $2k$ ideals $\mathfrak a_1,\ldots,\mathfrak a_k,
\mathfrak n_1,\ldots,\mathfrak n_k$, where the $\mathfrak a_\lambda$ are ideals from $\mR$ and the
$\mathfrak n_\lambda$ are ideals of the rational integers. Since for the $\mathfrak n_\lambda$
both the divisor-chain condition and the multiple-chain condition modulo every
nonzero ideal are satisfied, the proof for the divisor-chain condition remains valid
for $\overline{\mA}$ and hence for $\mA$; for the multiple-chain condition, however, one must
require that the $2k$ ideals assigned to $\mC$ respectively $\overline{\mC}$ are all different
from the zero ideal.


